  "about": "British and American English: words and spellings a reader of the book's region notices. A tool checks a text whose language tag is en-US for the gb forms and one tagged en-GB for the us forms; a bare `en` names no region and nothing is checked. Matching is by whole word or phrase, case-insensitive, with typographic apostrophes read as straight. `shared` lists the sides whose forms the other region also writes (autumn is literary American); `polysemous` the sides whose forms have another sense (fall, flat, post). A hit is `loose` when its form is either: never a correction, at most advice. Running text skips polysemous forms — flat the adjective is everywhere; a listing, read in a breath by the region's buyers, warns on all of them. `cases` are the contract: every tool's matcher returns exactly these hits, in text order.",
